On his last evening, Murugesan climbed a low green hill above the river and sat down on the grass as if it were his old terrace mat. Betelgeuse was setting, enormous and red, and the whole sky turned the colour of kumkum. A warm wind moved through the tall grass and made a sound like waves of paddy in the month of Thai.

He took out the small steel tumbler he had carried across the entire universe, filled it with water from the river, and raised it toward the sky, toward a yellow star too small to see, where a village outside Thanjavur was sleeping.

"Paati," he said softly, "I went. I saw the stars. All of them. And at the very end, I found a place that looks like home."

He smiled, lay back on the grass, and began, once again, to count the stars.

And somewhere far away, perhaps, the rasam was still warm.
